📘 Description

Depth First Search (DFS) is a graph/tree traversal algorithm used to visit all nodes by exploring one path completely before moving to another path. It uses a Stack (LIFO) data structure either explicitly or through Recursion.

DFS is widely used in:

Path finding 🛣️
Maze solving 🧩
Cycle detection 🔄
Topological sorting 📚
Artificial Intelligence 🤖
⚡ Characteristics of DFS

✔️ Traverses deeply before backtracking
✔️ Uses Stack or Recursion
✔️ Time Complexity → O(V + E)
✔️ Space Complexity → O(V)
✔️ Suitable for sparse graphs
✔️ May not give shortest path
✔️ Easy to implement recursively

Where:

V → Number of Vertices
E → Number of Edges
🔄 Working Process of DFS
Step-by-Step Process

1️⃣ Start from a source node
2️⃣ Mark the node as Visited ✅
3️⃣ Visit one unvisited adjacent node
4️⃣ Continue moving deeper ⬇️
5️⃣ If no unvisited node exists, backtrack 🔙
6️⃣ Repeat until all nodes are visited

🌳 Example Traversal

Graph:

      A
     / \
    B   C
   / \
  D   E

DFS Traversal:

A → B → D → E → C

